<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romanus Pontifex</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Romanus Pontifex is a papal bull, one of a series of fifteenth-century papal documents that granted Iberian crowns rights over newly encountered lands and peoples outside Christendom. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> cite it, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inter-caetera">Inter Caetera</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a>, as part of the legal and political foundation for dividing the non-Christian world between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal. Such bulls were used to justify conquest, colonisation and the enslavement of non-Christians.</p></div>
